.TH "ELEMENT.SETATTRIBUTENS" "3JS" "April 6, 2026" "JavaScript" "JavaScript Reference Manual"
.SH NAME
Element.setAttributeNS \- Element: setAttributeNS() method
.SH SYNOPSIS
.RS
.PP
\f[B]Warning:\f[R] This method can take attribute values that are parsed
as HTML, a script, or as a script URL, depending on the attribute.
APIs like this are known as injection sinks, and are potentially a
vector for cross\-site scripting (XSS) attacks, if the value originally
came from an attacker.
.PP
You can mitigate this risk by always passing the appropriate trusted
type object (\f[CR]TrustedHTML\f[R], \f[CR]TrustedScript\f[R], or
\f[CR]TrustedScriptURL\f[R]) instead of strings for those attributes
that require them, and enforcing trusted types.
See Security considerations in \f[CR]Element.setAttribute()\f[R] for
more information.
.RE
.PP
The \f[B]\f[CB]setAttributeNS()\f[B]\f[R] method of the
\f[CR]Element\f[R] interface adds a new attribute or changes the value
of an attribute with the given namespace and name.
.PP
If you are working with HTML documents and you don\(cqt need to specify
the requested attribute as being part of a specific namespace, use the
\f[CR]setAttribute()\f[R] method instead.
.SH SYNTAX
.IP
.EX
setAttributeNS(namespaceURI, qualifiedName, value)
.EE
.SS Parameters
.TP
\f[B]namespaceURI\f[R]
A string specifying the namespace of the attribute to set, or the empty
string.
.TP
\f[B]qualifiedName\f[R]
A string identifying the attribute by its qualified name, which has the
format \f[CR]prefix:localName\f[R] or \f[CR]localName\f[R], where the
parts are defined as:
.RS
.TP
\f[B]prefix\f[R]
A \(lqshort alias\(rq for the namespace.
The prefix is optional, but if it is specified the
\f[CR]namespaceURI\f[R] parameter must also be specified.
If the prefix is set to \f[CR]xml\f[R] or \f[CR]xmlns\f[R], the
\f[CR]namespaceURI\f[R] must be set to
\f[CR]http://www.w3.org/XML/1998/namespace\f[R] or
\f[CR]http://www.w3.org/2000/xmlns/\f[R], respectively.
.TP
\f[B]localName\f[R]
The local name of the attribute.
.RE
.TP
\f[B]value\f[R]
A trusted type or string containing the value to assign to the
attribute.
.RS
.PP
Trusted type instances must be passed for the following attributes when
trusted types are enforced:
.IP \(bu 2
Event handler content attributes, such as \f[CR]onclick\f[R] and
\f[CR]onload\f[R], require a \f[CR]TrustedScript\f[R].
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]HTMLIFrameElement.srcdoc\f[R] require a \f[CR]TrustedHTML\f[R]
instance.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]HTMLScriptElement.src\f[R] require a \f[CR]TrustedScriptURL\f[R]
instance.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]SVGScriptElement.href\f[R] require a \f[CR]TrustedScriptURL\f[R]
instance.
.PP
Trusted types are not enforced for other attributes, so a string or any
trusted type may be passed.
.RE
.SS Return value
None (\f[CR]undefined\f[R]).
.SS Exceptions
.TP
\f[B]NamespaceError\f[R] \f[B]DOMException\f[R]
Thrown if the \f[CR]namespaceURI\f[R] value is:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
not a valid namespace URI.
.IP \(bu 2
set to the empty string when \f[CR]prefix\f[R] has a value.
.IP \(bu 2
not the value \f[CR]http://www.w3.org/XML/1998/namespace\f[R] or
\f[CR]http://www.w3.org/2000/xmlns/\f[R] when \f[CR]prefix\f[R] is set
to \f[CR]xml\f[R] or \f[CR]xmlns\f[R], respectively.
.RE
.TP
\f[B]InvalidCharacterError\f[R] \f[B]DOMException\f[R]
Thrown if either the \f[CR]prefix\f[R] or \f[CR]localName\f[R] is not
valid:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
The \f[CR]prefix\f[R] must have at least one character, and cannot
contain ASCII whitespace, \f[CR]NULL\f[R], \f[CR]/\f[R], or \f[CR]>\f[R]
(U+0000, U+002F, or U+003E, respectively).
.IP \(bu 2
The \f[CR]localName\f[R] must have at least one character, and may not
contain ASCII whitespace, \f[CR]NULL\f[R], \f[CR]/\f[R], \f[CR]=\f[R] or
\f[CR]>\f[R] (U+0000, U+002F, U+003D, or U+003E, respectively).
.RS
.PP
\f[B]Note:\f[R] Earlier versions of the specification were more
restrictive, requiring that the \f[CR]qualifiedName\f[R] be a valid \c
.UR https://www.w3.org/TR/xml/#dt-name
XML name
.UE \c
\&.
.RE
.RE
.TP
\f[B]TypeError\f[R]
Thrown if \f[CR]value\f[R] is passed a string instead of a trusted type
object (for those attributes that require them) when Trusted Types are
enforced by a CSP and no default policy is defined.
.SH EXAMPLES
.SS Basic usage
.IP
.EX
\f[B]let\f[R] d = document.getElementById(\(dqd1\(dq);
d.setAttributeNS(
  \(dqhttp://www.mozilla.org/ns/specialspace\(dq,
  \(dqspec:align\(dq,
  \(dqcenter\(dq,
);
.EE
.SS Trusted types
The Setting unsafe attributes example in \f[CR]setAttribute()\f[R] shows
how you might use \f[CR]setAttributeNS()\f[R] with the trusted types.
.SH SEE ALSO
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]Element.hasAttributeNS()\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]Element.getAttributeNS()\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]Element.removeAttributeNS()\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]Element.setAttribute()\f[R]
